# `.crew/info.md` — who is in the crew, and who decides

> Copy this to `{{RECORDS_DIR}}/info.md` and fill it in. **Every agent reads this file fresh at the
> start of a session and again at every gate** — never from memory of an earlier read. It is the one
> place that says who is available, who decides, and what happens when someone is missing.
>
> Separating this from the skills is deliberate: the skills describe **the process**, this file
> describes **the authority**. That way you can change how much you delegate without editing a
> single skill.

---

## Mode

```
MODE: solo
```

Pick one. This single line changes what the process expects of you.

| Mode | Meaning | Use it when |
|---|---|---|
| **solo** | One agent does everything: plan, implement, review, validate | you only have one CLI agent, or you want simplicity over cross-checking |
| **duo** | Two complex agents. They plan/review each other's work. No delegated executors | you have Claude and Codex, but no `agy`/`copilot`, or you do not want delegation |
| **crew** | Full setup: two complex agents plus low-risk executors | everything is configured and the work has enough volume to delegate |

## Roster

Fill in only what exists. `(none)` is a valid, honest answer.

```
ORCHESTRATOR:   claude
SECOND COMPLEX: (none)
EXECUTORS:      (none)
BROWSER:        (none)
```

| Slot | What it is | Typical |
|---|---|---|
| `ORCHESTRATOR` | talks to the human, plans, splits, owns git | `claude` or `codex` |
| `SECOND COMPLEX` | reviews the orchestrator's plans and code | the other one |
| `EXECUTORS` | low-risk delegated work | `agy`, `copilot` |
| `BROWSER` | the Playwright MCP, for design review and runtime validation | `playwright` |

---

## 🔴 What `solo` mode changes — read this before assuming a review happened

In `solo`, **cross-review does not exist.** The kit's default policy is that nobody reviews their own
work, and in solo mode that policy cannot be satisfied by a second agent. Pretending otherwise is
worse than not having it, so the rules become:

| Normally | In solo |
|---|---|
| The other agent reviews the plan (`plan-review`) | **You review your own plan** — but on a separate pass, after writing it out in full, and you **declare in the verdict that it was a self-review** |
| The other agent reviews the code (`codereview`) | You review your own diff, **and you run your host's independent reviewers** — for Claude that means `/code-review` **and** `/security-review`. Those are not the same lens as yours, and in solo they are the only second opinion that exists |
| Delegated executors do the small work | You do all of it. Do not delegate to an executor that is not in the roster |
| `/execute-plan` fans out `implementer` agents | Still available — subagents of the same model are a weaker check than a different model, but they are a real one |

**In every solo report, state the mode.** A reader who does not know it was solo will assume a second
agent signed off, and that assumption is exactly how an unreviewed change ships with the appearance
of having been reviewed.

### What `duo` changes

Everything about review works normally. Delegation does not exist: every task is done by a complex
agent. Skip the `agy`/`copilot` sections of `mcp/README.md` entirely.

